Fix Available Services cache shrinking instead of growing (v0.136)

Dan noticed Settings > Couriers > DPD/APC > Available services only showed
a handful of codes (DPD: just "Two Day Parcel"; APC: just Next Day by
10/12/16) despite many more having appeared on real orders before.

Root cause: ApcShippingService.cacheLastKnownServices() and
DpdShippingService.cacheLastKnownServices() both overwrote the
apc_last_known_services/dpd_last_known_services Setting outright with
just the current live lookup's result, instead of merging it into
whatever was already cached. A single live lookup is scoped to one
order's weight/address (APC's ServiceAvailability.json only returns the
product families that fit that weight; DPD's outboundservices call is
similarly route-specific), so every lookup was quietly discarding
everything previously seen and replacing it with just that one order's
result - the cache (and its dead-API fallback, and the Available
Services toggle list, which reads the same cache) kept narrowing down to
whatever the most recently looked-up order happened to qualify for.

Fixed both to merge the new result into the existing cached list (keyed
by product code for APC, networkKey for DPD) rather than replacing it,
so a code already seen stays listed even when a later lookup only
returns a subset. There's no complete master catalog of every courier
service code anywhere in this app to reseed from (confirmed via project
docs - only a handful of fallback codes for APC, nothing for DPD), so
the lists that already got shrunk by the bug will rebuild back up
naturally as orders of varying weight/destination get looked up again,
rather than needing a one-off data fix.

Also: investigated Dan's GDMS recall report from his test order
yesterday - no code issue after all. Dan hadn't refreshed to the fixed
build before cancelling (so the recall genuinely didn't fire, as
expected pre-fix), then recalled manually through the GDMS portal
directly, which likely left the two systems' records disagreeing. He
resolved it himself by dispatching the same unit again (forcing a fresh
GDMS push) and recalling again, both of which worked. No further action
needed; noted in the project's GDMS findings doc for the record.

